EXT. DUST-BOWL GHOST TOWN - MAIN STREET - HIGH NOON

Wind scours the street. A tumbleweed rolls past a sagging saloon. A crooked sign reads "TIN CUP."

MARSHAL JEBEDIAH CROW, 50s, stands at one end of the street, a battered Colt on his hip. Opposite him, OLETA BAINBRIDGE, 40s, in a black duster, rests a hand on her holster. In the saloon doorway, SISTER MARGUERITE, 30s, a nun, clutches a lantern. On the roof of the livery, a boy, PETEY NAKAMURA, 14, lies hidden with a rifle. Behind a rain barrel crouches DEPUTY HOLLIS WAKEFIELD, 20s, sweating.

OLETA
You look older, Jeb.

JEBEDIAH
You look the same.

OLETA
That's the problem.

Marguerite steps down from the saloon porch, lantern swinging. Petey adjusts his aim. Hollis stands, then ducks.

SISTER MARGUERITE
Both of you, this is a house of God.

JEBEDIAH
It's a saloon, Sister.

SISTER MARGUERITE
It was a church first.

OLETA
Move aside, Sister.

SISTER MARGUERITE
I will not.

Oleta draws and fires. The lantern shatters. Marguerite screams. Jebediah draws. Petey's rifle cracks from the roof. Oleta's hat flies off. Hollis shouts and flings himself prone. A second shot echoes. Jebediah falls to one knee, hand to his shoulder.

JEBEDIAH
Who's up there?

OLETA
I thought he was yours.

JEBEDIAH
He's not mine.

PETEY
(from roof)
I'm nobody's!

Oleta looks at Jebediah. Jebediah looks at Oleta. She holsters her gun. He does not.

OLETA
Seems we've both been ambushed.

JEBEDIAH
Seems so.

SISTER MARGUERITE
(rising, grim)
Then come inside, the lot of you. And bring the boy.

Hollis crawls out from behind the barrel. Petey climbs down the ladder. Jebediah, bleeding, offers Oleta his arm. She takes it. They walk to the saloon together as the wind picks up.
